WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.379
Elimination tells us whether a

2
00:00:05.461 --> 00:00:07.775
square matrix is invertible

3
00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:09.262
count the pivots.

4
00:00:09.262 --> 00:00:12.815
Today we pack that answer into one number

5
00:00:12.815 --> 00:00:14.220
the determinant.

6
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:16.635
Start with the unit square

7
00:00:16.635 --> 00:00:19.770
spanned by the two rows of the identity.

8
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.920
Now take the matrix three, one, one, two.

9
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.350
Draw its rows as arrows, three, one

10
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:28.647
and one, two.

11
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.521
The square leans and stretches

12
00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:33.560
into a parallelogram.

13
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.144
How much area does it have?

14
00:00:36.144 --> 00:00:37.550
Pause and think.

15
00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.595
The area is five.

16
00:00:42.595 --> 00:00:45.870
The determinant is exactly this area

17
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:49.490
with a sign, and three rules pin it down.

18
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.584
Rule one: the identity has determinant one.

19
00:00:53.584 --> 00:00:55.760
That is the unit square.

20
00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:56.952
Rule two

21
00:00:56.952 --> 00:01:00.600
exchanging two rows reverses the sign.

22
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.688
The robot's moves have rows one

23
00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:06.120
two and three, minus one.

24
00:01:06.214 --> 00:01:08.927
Turning from the first row to

25
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.452
the second goes clockwise

26
00:01:11.452 --> 00:01:15.569
so the area of seven counts as minus seven.

27
00:01:15.569 --> 00:01:19.030
Swap the rows, and it is plus seven.

28
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:20.366
Rule three

29
00:01:20.366 --> 00:01:23.860
the determinant is linear in each row

30
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.728
with the other rows held fixed.

31
00:01:26.728 --> 00:01:29.147
Back to our parallelogram.

32
00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:32.732
Double the first row: the area doubles

33
00:01:32.732 --> 00:01:33.449
to ten.

34
00:01:33.449 --> 00:01:35.420
Halve it again: five.

35
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.386
Linear also means sums split

36
00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:40.281
if one row is a sum

37
00:01:40.371 --> 00:01:42.717
the determinant is the sum

38
00:01:42.807 --> 00:01:44.611
of two determinants.

39
00:01:44.611 --> 00:01:46.957
And here is the surprise

40
00:01:46.957 --> 00:01:50.927
exactly one function obeys all three rules

41
00:01:50.927 --> 00:01:52.370
for every size.

42
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.254
Test the rules on a d minus b c.

43
00:01:56.254 --> 00:01:58.731
The identity gives one.

44
00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:02.964
Exchanging the rows gives c b minus d a

45
00:02:02.964 --> 00:02:04.410
the negative.

46
00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:08.023
And multiplying the first row by t

47
00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:11.430
multiplies the whole thing by t.

48
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.236
Rule three is easy to misread.

49
00:02:14.236 --> 00:02:17.575
If doubling one row doubles the determinant

50
00:02:17.649 --> 00:02:19.504
surely doubling the whole

51
00:02:19.578 --> 00:02:21.210
matrix doubles it too?

52
00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.847
Double the whole matrix

53
00:02:23.847 --> 00:02:25.733
and both rows double.

54
00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:27.961
The parallelogram doubles

55
00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:30.019
in width and in height

56
00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:33.619
so its area is four times as big: twenty

57
00:02:33.619 --> 00:02:34.390
not ten.

58
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.627
Each row that doubles brings

59
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.029
its own factor of two: two rows

60
00:02:41.029 --> 00:02:42.885
so two times two.

61
00:02:42.885 --> 00:02:46.391
In general, for an n by n matrix

62
00:02:46.494 --> 00:02:48.969
t times A has t to the n

63
00:02:49.072 --> 00:02:51.340
times the determinant.

64
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.222
Now three consequences of the rules.

65
00:02:55.222 --> 00:02:58.799
Number four: two equal rows give zero.

66
00:02:58.891 --> 00:03:01.551
As the second row swings onto

67
00:03:01.642 --> 00:03:03.660
the line of the first

68
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.320
the parallelogram goes flat.

69
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:10.243
The rules say why: exchange two equal rows

70
00:03:10.243 --> 00:03:12.819
and the matrix stays the same

71
00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:14.814
yet the sign must flip.

72
00:03:14.814 --> 00:03:18.470
A number equal to its own negative is zero.

73
00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:19.748
Number five

74
00:03:19.826 --> 00:03:22.379
subtracting a multiple of one row

75
00:03:22.456 --> 00:03:24.699
from another changes nothing.

76
00:03:24.699 --> 00:03:28.026
Take a third of row one away from row two

77
00:03:28.026 --> 00:03:29.960
the parallelogram leans

78
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.971
but base and height stay

79
00:03:31.971 --> 00:03:34.060
so the area is still five.

80
00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:38.683
Now the matrix is triangular: three, one

81
00:03:38.683 --> 00:03:40.663
zero, five thirds.

82
00:03:40.663 --> 00:03:44.935
One more lean turns it into a rectangle

83
00:03:44.935 --> 00:03:48.374
three wide and five thirds high.

84
00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:51.605
So a triangular matrix has the

85
00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:54.210
product of its diagonal.

86
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.259
And number six: a row of zeros gives zero.

87
00:03:58.259 --> 00:04:00.902
Shrink a row towards nothing

88
00:04:00.902 --> 00:04:03.280
and the area goes with it.

89
00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.108
Two quick ones, with no computing at all.

90
00:04:07.108 --> 00:04:10.038
Exchange the two rows of our matrix

91
00:04:10.038 --> 00:04:11.068
by rule two

92
00:04:11.068 --> 00:04:13.919
the determinant becomes minus five.

93
00:04:13.999 --> 00:04:16.216
Multiply the original second

94
00:04:16.296 --> 00:04:19.147
row by three instead: by rule three

95
00:04:19.147 --> 00:04:19.860
fifteen.

96
00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:23.292
And a triangular matrix with a zero on

97
00:04:23.373 --> 00:04:26.130
its diagonal has determinant zero

98
00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.211
even though none of its rows is zero

99
00:04:29.211 --> 00:04:31.644
both rows point the same way

100
00:04:31.644 --> 00:04:35.050
and elimination would produce a zero row.

101
00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.134
So the determinant is a signed area.

102
00:04:39.134 --> 00:04:42.248
Rule one fixes the unit square

103
00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:44.097
rule two the sign

104
00:04:44.097 --> 00:04:47.990
and rule three the scaling of each row.

105
00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.497
Elimination steps only lean the shape

106
00:04:51.497 --> 00:04:53.075
so the area stays.

107
00:04:53.075 --> 00:04:55.152
Exchanges flip the sign

108
00:04:55.152 --> 00:04:58.060
and equal rows flatten it to zero.

109
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.696
In part two, the pivots give a fast way to

110
00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.514
compute any determinant

111
00:05:03.587 --> 00:05:06.643
and the determinant of a product turns out

112
00:05:06.716 --> 00:05:09.480
to be the product of the determinants.
